SPU: a DMA into a resident code span takes over only the bytes it wrote, not the whole span.

A GET from a source other than the span's own load dropped the span (spu_overlay_note_get), so every later dispatch in
it went to the interpreter. Resistance 3's Insomniac job modules at LS 0x100 have their data, and the dispatcher's
buffers for its smaller modules (0x6600, 0x6780, 0xDF00), inside their span: the first data DMA of a job dropped the
module, and the rest of that job ran in the interpreter (a census of the interpreter's steps: nearly all at
0x2D00..0x6C00 and 0x12B00 of modules whose span had been dropped). Now each span keeps the ranges other DMAs wrote
since its load (up to SPU_RESIDENT_CODE_HOLES; more merge into the last one, which only ever covers more). Inside such
a range the address resolves as if the span were not resident (a span loaded there, then task, overlay, image), as all
of the span did before; elsewhere the span's lift runs, and its bytes there are still the load's. Verification of a
verified region compares the bytes outside the ranges. A load of the span's own module clears them.

Test: runtime/spu/tests/test_spu_lifted_start.c test_resident_code_holes, test_verified_with_holes and the adapted
case in test_verified_resident_code_regions (6 checks fail without the change). Scripted run, together with 0006 and
the buffer modules of scripts/extract_spu_raw.py: interpreter steps in gameplay about 23M/s -> 13M/s (what is left is
mostly the position-independent modules); Home level, stick turning, two runs: 24.2 -> 26.6-26.9 fps.

Upstream: not proposed yet (same problem as the code-only spans of PR #444, for spans whose code end is not known).
